Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 07:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 106/106] ipa: simple: Give AfWindows a usable range, and honour
 AfMetering
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

AfWindows is advertised with both bounds set to the empty rectangle:

  Control: [inout] libcamera::AfWindows: [(0, 0)/0x0..(0, 0)/0x0]

so an application reading the control to find out what it may ask for is
told that the only permitted window is one with no area. The algorithm
behind it can already meter an arbitrary window - selectZones() has been
there since the autofocus went in - but nothing says where those windows
live, which is the one thing the application cannot work out for itself.

The bounds cannot be filled in at configure() time as the comment they
replace intended: the ControlInfoMap a camera advertises is taken once,
when the software ISP constructs the IPA, and is never read again. The
sensor information is passed to init() before the algorithms are created,
so state them there, where they are both available and effective.

The space is the sensor's active pixel array, and not - as an earlier
version of this patch had it - the software ISP's output frame. The
output size is the one thing about the sensor that moves: the IPA is
initialised with whatever V4L2 format the sensor was left in, and a V4L2
format outlives the process that applied it, so advertising it tells an
application to compute tap positions in the space the previous user of
the camera happened to leave behind. Measured on a Fairphone 3, the
control read (0, 0)/1920x1080 after a 1080p capture and (0, 0)/4032x3024
after a full-resolution one, with nothing but the earlier session between
them. The active area is a property of the hardware and read the same
either way. selectZones() maps a window onto the zones proportionally and
so follows it without needing an absolute size of its own; that mapping
assumes the stream covers the active area, which a sensor mode that
cropped rather than scaled would break, and correcting for that would
need analogCrop - known only for the format the IPA was initialised with,
and therefore no more dependable here than the output size.

Note this deviates from the control's documentation, which puts AfWindows
in ScalerCropMaximum pixels: the simple pipeline handler publishes no
such property, so there would be nothing for an application to read. The
control's own maximum states the space unambiguously instead, and on a
pipeline that cannot crop the two coincide.

While here, derive the metered zones from AfMetering and AfWindows
together rather than from whichever arrives last. The two controls are
independent and may be set in either order, and acting on each where it
is read gets both orders wrong: windows were applied even under
AfMeteringAuto, contrary to their documented meaning, and switching to
AfMeteringWindows after windows had been given left the metering on the
whole frame. Keeping both as requested and recomputing from the pair
makes the result independent of the order.

selectZones() now reports whether it selected anything instead of quietly
metering the whole frame when it did not, which is the one thing a caller
asking for windowed metering did not ask for; windowed metering with no
usable window falls back to the centre, as it already did when no window
had been given at all.

Where the score is metered from is otherwise invisible from outside the
IPA, so log how many zones ended up selected: a window that reached
nothing is indistinguishable from one that was never sent.

A single window may arrive as a scalar Rectangle rather than as a
one-element array - libcamera's own cam tool sends it that way - so read
the raw ControlValue and accept both shapes. Reading a scalar through
the array accessor asserts and aborts the IPA process, which nothing had
ever hit because no window could reach this code before.

